  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to enhance the optimization process by combining a more sophisticated initialization strategy with a hybrid optimization technique. The existing approach uses a simple step function as an initial guess, which may lead to suboptimal solutions due to local minima. By using a randomized initial configuration with controlled spreading of 1s and employing a combination of local and global search strategies, we can improve the chances of finding a lower C\u2085 bound. This approach addresses the non-convexity challenge through perturbation-based exploration and constrained optimization.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation: A step function h(x) discretized into n_points intervals with dx = 2/n_points. \nVariables: h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] for all i, and \u2211h[i] = n_points/2. \nObjective: Minimize max_k [\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx] where k \u2208 [-2, 2]. \nConstraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h[i] = n_points/2. \nEvaluation: Compute np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode=\"full\") * dx, and take the maximum value across all lags. \nSurrogate: No direct convex relaxation; use gradient-based methods with constraints enforced through projection. \nSolver: SLSQP with constraints and bounds, supplemented by random perturbations. \n\n3. Implementation procedure\nInput: n_points (e.g., 200), seed. \nInitialization: Generate a random mask with exactly n_points/2 ones, spread evenly across the interval. \nProcedure:\n- Normalize the initial mask to meet the sum constraint.\n- Use SLSQP optimization with bounds and equality constraint for sum.\n- Introduce random perturbations to the solution at regular intervals to escape local optima.\n- After each optimization step, clip values to [0, 1] and re-normalize sum.\n- Monitor time budget, and if exhausted, return the best solution found.\n- Use a secondary optimizer (e.g., L-BFGS-B) for refinement after global exploration. \nOutputs: Normalized h_values (list), computed C\u2085, and n_points. \n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration: n_points = 200, initial seed. \nInitialization: Use np.random.choice with replacement to generate positions for ones, ensuring even distribution. \nOptimization: Alternate between SLSQP and L-BFGS-B with adaptive tolerances. \nPerturbation: Add random noise (\u00b10.05) to h_values after every 10 iterations. \nVariations: \n- Use different n_points (e.g., 300) for higher resolution. \n- Try different initial distributions (e.g., sine wave patterns). \n- Switch optimization methods based on progress toward the objective. \n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime estimation: 600 seconds for 200-point optimization with 300 iterations. \nMemory: ~100 MB for arrays and solver states. \nDeadline management: Insert time checks in the optimization loop to terminate early if budget is exhausted. \nRobustness: \n- Clip h_values to [0, 1] after every update. \n- Re-normalize sum to ensure feasibility. \n- Use the initial_h_values as a fallback if no improvement is detected. \n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks: \n- All h_values must be in [0, 1]. \n- Sum of h_values must equal n_points/2. \nObjective consistency: Re-evaluate the correlation using the original task metric. \nSanity checks: \n- Verify that the returned h_values meet the constraints. \n- Confirm that the computed C\u2085 is finite and matches the evaluation logic. \nReturn format: (h_values.tolist(), computed C\u2085, n_points).",
